For \(\mathcal C\in\{\mathrm{NI},\mathrm{SI}\}\), let \(\mathfrak K_{\mathcal C}(n,d,\varepsilon)\) be the same one-person-one-message protocol class as \(\mathfrak Q_{\mathcal C}\), with original input alphabet replaced by \(\mathcal A_d\). Explicitly, a protocol \(\mathsf K\) consists of an arbitrary standard-Borel public-seed space with a law \(\lambda\), standard-Borel message spaces, and kernels \(\mathsf K_i\) satisfying
\[
 \mathsf K_i(E\mid(j,s),\eta,r)
 \le e^\varepsilon \mathsf K_i(E\mid(j',s'),\eta,r)
\]
for every participant, seed, history, Borel message event, \(j,j'\in[d]\) and \(s,s'\in\{-1,1\}\). Its choices depend only on \(n,d,\varepsilon\), the specified model and public bounds. For NI, every kernel is constant in its history; nonidentical index kernels and shared public seeds are allowed. For SI the kernels can depend on the previous messages and access each participant once. Let \(\mathbf Z^{\mathsf K}\) denote the message vector. In both models \(R\sim\lambda\) and \(U\sim\operatorname{Uniform}[0,1]\) are mutually independent and independent of the iid inputs. Write \(\widehat D\) for any Borel real-valued decision based only on \((\mathbf Z^{\mathsf K},R,U)\), and \(J_{\mathrm{TV}}\) for any nonempty connected closed interval in \([0,1]\) with ordered Borel endpoints based on that same information. For either model \(\mathcal M\) from the preceding definition, set
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \mathfrak R^{\mathrm{TV}}_{\mathcal C}(\mathcal M;n,d,\varepsilon)
 &=\inf_{\mathsf K\in\mathfrak K_{\mathcal C}(n,d,\varepsilon)}
     \inf_{\widehat D}\sup_{\mathsf p\in\mathcal M}
       \mathbb E_{\mathsf p,\mathsf K}\bigl[(\widehat D-D_{\mathrm{TV}}(\mathsf p))^2\bigr],\\
 \mathfrak H^{\mathrm{TV}}_{\mathcal C}(\mathcal M;n,d,\varepsilon)
 &=\inf_{\mathsf K\in\mathfrak K_{\mathcal C}(n,d,\varepsilon)}
   \inf_{J_{\mathrm{TV}}:\,
      \inf_{\mathsf p\in\mathcal M}\Pr_{\mathsf p,\mathsf K}\{D_{\mathrm{TV}}(\mathsf p)\in J_{\mathrm{TV}}\}\ge0.90}
       \sup_{\mathsf p\in\mathcal M}\mathbb E_{\mathsf p,\mathsf K}\operatorname{length}J_{\mathrm{TV}}.
 \end{aligned}
\]
The probabilities and expectations include input sampling, channel randomness, public randomness and analyst randomization. Singletons are permitted. In the paired model the attaining intervals below are contained in the sharper range \([0,1/4]\).
